        "title": "Database Observability: Query Logging & Slow Query Traps",
        "language": "sql",
        "starterCode": "-- PostgreSQL slow query log threshold\nSET log_min_duration_statement = '250ms';\n\n-- Inspect active database locks and long-running transactions\nSELECT pid, now() - xact_start AS duration, query, state\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE state != 'idle'\nORDER BY duration DESC;\n",
        "content": "<h2>☕ Database Observability: Query Logging & Slow Query Diagnostics</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 Enterprise Invariant & Best Practice:</strong> <em>A high-performance enterprise database requires 24/7 observability. Diagnose slow queries using pg_stat_activity, pg_stat_statements, and log thresholds.</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>Production performance degradation is rarely sudden; it accumulates as tables grow and un-indexed queries consume database CPU. Establishing database observability allows you to identify and remediate bottlenecks before users notice.</p>\n  <p>PostgreSQL provides <code>pg_stat_statements</code>, an extension that records execution counts, total runtime, min/max/mean timings, and memory buffer usage for all executed query templates. Pair this with <code>log_min_duration_statement = '250ms'</code> to automatically record slow queries to disk logs for analysis.</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Architecture Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\nSELECT \n    calls, round(total_exec_time::numeric, 2) as total_ms,\n    round(mean_exec_time::numeric, 2) as avg_ms, query\nFROM pg_stat_statements\nORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 5;\n\nResult: Identifies the top 5 CPU-consuming SQL queries across the entire cluster!\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Enterprise Best Practices & Production Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>pg_stat_activity:</strong> Query pg_stat_activity to inspect currently running transactions, active locks, and detect blocked queries in real time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Statement Timeout:</strong> Configure statement_timeout = 30000ms to automatically abort queries running longer than 30 seconds, preventing runaway lock exhaustion.</li>\n  <li><strong>Connection Saturation:</strong> Monitor pg_stat_database for connection count spikes indicating connection pool leaks in microservices.</li>\n</ul>"
